    {
      "id": "tjY1kDE61u2wPlHqI45Kl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1600.5958829987187,
      "y": 2682.607261923575,
      "width": 458.6799082091979,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 474401488,
      "version": 228,
      "versionNonce": 302732496,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786749802434,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "** a DB rollback cannot roll back external side\neffects such as an S3 upload, Kafka publish,\nor email. For those, use patterns like\ntransactional outbox, idempotency keys, or\ncompensating actions.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "** a DB rollback cannot roll back external side effects such as an S3 upload, Kafka publish, or email. For those, use patterns like transactional outbox, idempotency keys, or compensating actions.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "xe-t4XHUT2z9VmkQ3BkRp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1200.2186805708304,
      "y": 2865.2536009686455,
      "width": 864.9724811898532,
      "height": 2200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 127036624,
      "version": 340,
      "versionNonce": 2012738096,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786749960611,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "* Storage model and scope: define what is being managed—objects, files, database rows,\nsnapshots, backups—and whether quota applies per user, project, tenant, organization, or\nresource type.\n\n* Source of truth: separate metadata from blob data. Blob bytes live in object/file storage;\nmetadata lives in a transactional store with fields like resource_id, tenant_id, size, state,\nstorage_class, checksum, version, and timestamps.\n\n* Quota enforcement: do not calculate quota by scanning storage on every upload. Maintain a\nusage counter or reservation ledger. Before a write, perform an atomic reserve(requestedBytes)\noperation; after upload completes, convert the reservation into committed usage. Expired or\nfailed reservations are released.\n\n* Concurrency correctness: quota updates must be atomic. Use row locking, optimistic\nversioning, compare-and-swap, or atomic counters depending on the database. The key\ninvariant is committed + reserved <= quota.\n\n* Upload flow: for large files, the API should create an upload session and return a presigned\nURL. The client uploads directly to object storage so application servers do not proxy gigabytes\nof data.\n\n* Two-phase lifecycle: model states such as PENDING -> UPLOADING -> AVAILABLE, with\nFAILED, DELETING, and DELETED. Avoid treating a blob as usable until metadata and storage\nverification complete.\n\n* Partial failure handling: a database rollback cannot undo an S3 write. Use reconciliation and\ncompensating actions. For example, if the blob exists but metadata commit fails, mark or detect\nit as an orphan and clean it asynchronously.\n\n* Idempotency: every create/delete/move request should carry an idempotency key so retries do\nnot allocate quota twice or delete twice.\n\n* Delete semantics: usually use logical delete first, release quota according to product\nsemantics, then physically delete asynchronously. If recovery is required, keep a trash window\nbefore permanent deletion.\n\n* Consistency choice: quota enforcement often needs strong consistency at the tenant\nboundary, while dashboards and analytics can be eventually consistent. I would explicitly\nseparate the enforcement path from the reporting path.\n\n* Hot tenants: one large tenant can make a single quota row a contention point. Partition usage\nby resource type or bucket, use reservation shards, or aggregate local counters into a strongly\nconsistent tenant-level budget.\n\n* Async accounting: storage systems emit object-created/object-deleted events into\nKafka/Pub/Sub. Workers update secondary usage views, billing aggregates, cleanup state, and\naudit logs.\n\n* Reconciliation: never trust events alone. Run periodic jobs comparing metadata DB state\nagainst actual object storage inventory to detect missing objects, orphan blobs, incorrect sizes,\nand drift.\n\n* Storage classes: hot/warm/cold/archive tiers should be policy-driven. Lifecycle workers can\nmove old data to cheaper storage without changing the logical resource identifier.\n\n* Deduplication: content hashes can reduce physical storage, but be careful: logical quota and\nphysical storage cost are not necessarily the same thing. One 1 GB object referenced by ten\ntenants might be 10 GB of logical usage but only 1 GB physical.\n\n* Versioning: decide whether old versions count toward quota. This affects user expectations,\nbilling, and cleanup substantially.\n\n* Multi-region strategy: metadata may need a clear write leader or tenant-home region. Blob\nstorage can replicate asynchronously. Avoid global synchronous quota writes unless the\nproduct truly requires them.\n\n* Security: authorization must be checked at metadata access and URL issuance. Presigned\nURLs should be short-lived and scoped to a specific object, operation, size, and content type\nwhere possible.\n\n* Tenant isolation: namespace object keys by tenant, enforce policies server-side, and never\ntrust a tenant ID sent by the client without validating it against authenticated identity.\n\n* Encryption: encrypt data in transit and at rest; consider per-tenant KMS keys for higher\nisolation requirements.\n\n* Abuse protection: rate limits, object-count limits, maximum file size, multipart limits, upload\nTTLs, and malware/content scanning may be required even if byte quota is available.\n\n* Observability: track reserved bytes, committed bytes, orphan objects, failed uploads, delete\nbacklog, reconciliation drift, quota rejection rate, upload latency, storage cost, lifecycle\ntransitions, and event lag.\n\n* SLOs: distinguish control-plane SLOs from data-plane SLOs. For example, quota check might\ntarget tens of milliseconds while a multi-GB upload naturally takes much longer.\n\n* Cost: storage cost is not only bytes. Include API calls, replication, egress, lifecycle transition\nfees, indexing, backups, and cross-region transfer.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "* Storage model and scope: define what is being managed—objects, files, database rows, snapshots, backups—and whether quota applies per user, project, tenant, organization, or resource type.\n\n* Source of truth: separate metadata from blob data. Blob bytes live in object/file storage; metadata lives in a transactional store with fields like resource_id, tenant_id, size, state, storage_class, checksum, version, and timestamps.\n\n* Quota enforcement: do not calculate quota by scanning storage on every upload. Maintain a usage counter or reservation ledger. Before a write, perform an atomic reserve(requestedBytes) operation; after upload completes, convert the reservation into committed usage. Expired or failed reservations are released.\n\n* Concurrency correctness: quota updates must be atomic. Use row locking, optimistic versioning, compare-and-swap, or atomic counters depending on the database. The key invariant is committed + reserved <= quota.\n\n* Upload flow: for large files, the API should create an upload session and return a presigned URL. The client uploads directly to object storage so application servers do not proxy gigabytes of data.\n\n* Two-phase lifecycle: model states such as PENDING -> UPLOADING -> AVAILABLE, with FAILED, DELETING, and DELETED. Avoid treating a blob as usable until metadata and storage verification complete.\n\n* Partial failure handling: a database rollback cannot undo an S3 write. Use reconciliation and compensating actions. For example, if the blob exists but metadata commit fails, mark or detect it as an orphan and clean it asynchronously.\n\n* Idempotency: every create/delete/move request should carry an idempotency key so retries do not allocate quota twice or delete twice.\n\n* Delete semantics: usually use logical delete first, release quota according to product semantics, then physically delete asynchronously. If recovery is required, keep a trash window before permanent deletion.\n\n* Consistency choice: quota enforcement often needs strong consistency at the tenant boundary, while dashboards and analytics can be eventually consistent. I would explicitly separate the enforcement path from the reporting path.\n\n* Hot tenants: one large tenant can make a single quota row a contention point. Partition usage by resource type or bucket, use reservation shards, or aggregate local counters into a strongly consistent tenant-level budget.\n\n* Async accounting: storage systems emit object-created/object-deleted events into Kafka/Pub/Sub. Workers update secondary usage views, billing aggregates, cleanup state, and audit logs.\n\n* Reconciliation: never trust events alone. Run periodic jobs comparing metadata DB state against actual object storage inventory to detect missing objects, orphan blobs, incorrect sizes, and drift.\n\n* Storage classes: hot/warm/cold/archive tiers should be policy-driven. Lifecycle workers can move old data to cheaper storage without changing the logical resource identifier.\n\n* Deduplication: content hashes can reduce physical storage, but be careful: logical quota and physical storage cost are not necessarily the same thing. One 1 GB object referenced by ten tenants might be 10 GB of logical usage but only 1 GB physical.\n\n* Versioning: decide whether old versions count toward quota. This affects user expectations, billing, and cleanup substantially.\n\n* Multi-region strategy: metadata may need a clear write leader or tenant-home region. Blob storage can replicate asynchronously. Avoid global synchronous quota writes unless the product truly requires them.\n\n* Security: authorization must be checked at metadata access and URL issuance. Presigned URLs should be short-lived and scoped to a specific object, operation, size, and content type where possible.\n\n* Tenant isolation: namespace object keys by tenant, enforce policies server-side, and never trust a tenant ID sent by the client without validating it against authenticated identity.\n\n* Encryption: encrypt data in transit and at rest; consider per-tenant KMS keys for higher isolation requirements.\n\n* Abuse protection: rate limits, object-count limits, maximum file size, multipart limits, upload TTLs, and malware/content scanning may be required even if byte quota is available.\n\n* Observability: track reserved bytes, committed bytes, orphan objects, failed uploads, delete backlog, reconciliation drift, quota rejection rate, upload latency, storage cost, lifecycle transitions, and event lag.\n\n* SLOs: distinguish control-plane SLOs from data-plane SLOs. For example, quota check might target tens of milliseconds while a multi-GB upload naturally takes much longer.\n\n* Cost: storage cost is not only bytes. Include API calls, replication, egress, lifecycle transition fees, indexing, backups, and cross-region transfer.",
